{
  "id": "chem-004",
  "title": "Why is the ocean salty?",
  "category": "chemistry",
  "subject_area": "dissolved salts and minerals",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "Rivers are fresh water, so why is the ocean salty?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "Salts and minerals dissolved from rocks on land and from vents in the seafloor flow into the ocean and stay there, so the ocean has become salty.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Seawater on Earth; the amount of salt in a place changes with rain, rivers, evaporation and ice.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Rain falls on the land and wears away the rocks, and it carries tiny bits of salts and minerals into streams and rivers. Rivers do not taste salty, because rain keeps refilling them with fresh water. But all the rivers run into the ocean, and the ocean keeps the salts they bring. Over a very long time the ocean has become salty.",
    "level_2": "Rainwater is slightly acidic, so it erodes rocks, and it carries salts and minerals as dissolved ions into streams and rivers and then the ocean. A second source is hydrothermal vents: seawater seeps into the seafloor, is heated, dissolves minerals and flows back out through the vents. Some dissolved ions are used by ocean life or removed from the water, but others are not, so their concentration builds up over time.",
    "level_3": "Concentration is the mass of dissolved salt divided by the volume of the water, c = m / V, so adding salt to the ocean without adding the same amount of water makes it saltier. Rivers and streams carry about four billion tons of dissolved salts to the ocean every year. The primeval seas were probably only slightly salty, and they became saltier as rain broke up rocks and carried their minerals to the ocean. Hydrothermal vents also matter: the entire volume of the oceans could circulate through them every 10 to 20 million years."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Over time the ocean has become saltier as rain fell and ran over the land, breaking up rocks and carrying their minerals to the sea.",
    "The entire volume of the oceans could circulate through hydrothermal vents every 10 to 20 million years."
  ],
